        "paragraphs": [
          {
            "index": 0,
            "blockIndex": 0,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "We can now describe **electromagnetic induction** quantitatively.\nFaraday's experiments showed that the induced current $I_i$ in a conducting loop is proportional to how quickly the number of field lines of $\\vec{B}$ passing through the loop's area changes.\nThe idea of **magnetic flux** $\\Phi$ lets us state this result more precisely."
          },
          {
            "index": 1,
            "blockIndex": 1,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "**Magnetic flux** measures how much magnetic field passes through an area $S$;\nthe number of field lines through that area is a visual way to represent it.\nThe rate at which this number changes represents the rate of change of magnetic flux."
          },
          {
            "index": 2,
            "blockIndex": 2,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "During a very short time $\\Delta t$, suppose the flux changes by $\\Delta\\Phi$.\nIts rate of change is $\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{\\Delta t}$, so the experimental result can be stated as follows:"
          },
          {
            "index": 3,
            "blockIndex": 3,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "**The induced current is proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux through the loop's area:**"
          },
          {
            "index": 4,
            "blockIndex": 4,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "$I_i\\propto\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{\\Delta t}$. (12-3)"
          },
          {
            "index": 5,
            "blockIndex": 5,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "A current can flow when non-electrostatic forces act on the free charges in a conductor.\nThe work done by these forces per unit positive charge as it travels around a closed loop is called **electromotive force**, or **EMF**."
          },
          {
            "index": 6,
            "blockIndex": 5,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "A change in the magnetic flux through the loop also produces this action.\nThe EMF associated with it is called **induced EMF**, written as $\\mathcal{E}_i$."
          },
          {
            "index": 7,
            "blockIndex": 6,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Ohm's law for the complete circuit gives $I_i=\\frac{\\mathcal{E}_i}{R}$.\nHere, the resistance of the conducting loop does not change as the magnetic flux changes.\nRelation (12-3) therefore holds because the induced EMF is proportional to $\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{\\Delta t}$."
          },
          {
            "index": 8,
            "blockIndex": 7,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "**The law of electromagnetic induction describes EMF, rather than current**.\nThis states the essential relationship without making it depend on the properties of the conductor carrying the induced current.\nThe law says: **the magnitude of the induced EMF in a closed loop equals the absolute value of the rate of change of magnetic flux through its area**①:"
          },
          {
            "index": 9,
            "blockIndex": 8,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "① Experiments directly establish only that $\\mathcal{E}_i$ is proportional to $\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{\\Delta t}$.\nIf the unit of magnetic flux has not yet been fixed, it can be chosen so that the proportionality constant equals 1."
          },
          {
            "index": 10,
            "blockIndex": 9,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "$|\\mathcal{E}_i|=\\left|\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{\\Delta t}\\right|$."
          },
          {
            "index": 11,
            "blockIndex": 10,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "How can we include the direction of the induced current, or the sign of the induced EMF, as given by **Lenz's law**?"
          },
          {
            "index": 12,
            "blockIndex": 11,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "Figure 286 shows a circular loop.\nWe choose the following positive direction: when the field $\\vec{B}'$ produced by the induced current inside the loop points in the same direction as the field $\\vec{B}$ that causes the induction, we call the current and the EMF **positive**."
          },
          {
            "index": 13,
            "blockIndex": 11,
            "paragraphInBlock": 1,
            "text": "By Lenz's law, the fields $\\vec{B}'$ and $\\vec{B}$ point in the same direction when the flux causing the induction is decreasing: that is, $\\Delta\\Phi<0$, and therefore $\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{\\Delta t}<0$.\nFor the induced EMF to be positive under our convention in this case, the law must include a **minus sign**:"
          },
          {
            "index": 14,
            "blockIndex": 14,
            "paragraphInBlock": 0,
            "text": "$\\mathcal{E}_i=-\\frac{\\Delta\\Phi}{\\Delta t}$. (12-4)"
          }
        ],
